      "writtenOn": "2018-12-12T17:30:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "You\u0027re right, __init__() is likely the first logical place to set `self.log`, but the requirement is self.log is set before any code in the syncstep class emits a log message. If they\u0027re only printed as a side_effect of `sync_record` and/or `delete_record` then the requirement is covered. If some code is printing a log message as a side_effect of `__init__` itself or some other function (`fetch_pending`?) then it would be a problem for it to be uninitialized at that time.\n\nThe safest approach may be for event_loop.py to pass a `log` object to __init__ when instantiating the step, and SyncStep.__init__ to set self.log from that. If each descendant class calls the base with **args, then the descendants are agnostic to how the logger was passed in. Seems like this would be better practice than what event_loop.py does now, which is to externally manipulate variables from outside the object.",
